			<section id="age-guide" class="age-guide-section">
				<div class="container">
					<div class="guide-intro">
						<div>
							<p class="eyebrow">Age calculation guide</p>
							<h2>Everything you need to know about age calculation</h2>
						</div>

						<p>
							An age calculator looks simple on the surface, but an accurate result
							has to respect real calendar rules. Months have different lengths,
							leap years add an extra day, and calendar traditions do not all begin
							their years on the same date. This guide explains what the numbers on
							this page mean and how the calculator arrives at them.
						</p>
					</div>

					<nav class="guide-jump-links" aria-label="Age calculator guide">
						<a href="#what-is-an-age-calculator">What it does</a>
						<a href="#how-age-is-calculated">How age is calculated</a>
						<a href="#leap-years">Leap years</a>
						<a href="#age-units">Age in different units</a>
						<a href="#birth-traditions-guide">Birth traditions</a>
					</nav>

					<div class="guide-feature-grid">
						<article id="what-is-an-age-calculator" class="guide-card guide-card-wide">
							<div class="guide-number" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
							<div>
								<h3>What is an age calculator?</h3>
								<p>
									An age calculator measures the time between a birth date and a
									reference date, normally today. A useful calculator does more than
									subtract one year number from another. It works out how many complete
									years have passed, how many complete calendar months followed those
									years, and how many days remain after that.
								</p>
								<p>
									That distinction matters. Someone born late in the year has not
									reached the same age as someone born early in the year simply because
									the current year minus the birth year produces the same number. Their
									birthday has to occur first. This calculator therefore treats age as
									calendar time rather than a rough difference between two year values.
								</p>
								<p>
									The result is then expanded into other useful views: total weeks,
									days, hours, minutes and seconds lived, the next birthday countdown,
									the weekday of birth, Hijri date, Western zodiac sign and Chinese
									zodiac. Those extra results all begin with the same selected birth
									date, but they answer different questions about it.
								</p>
							</div>
						</article>

						<article id="how-age-is-calculated" class="guide-card">
							<div class="guide-number" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
							<h3>How exact age is calculated</h3>
							<p>
								The calculator starts with the current date on your device and your
								selected date of birth. It first finds the difference between the two
								years. If your birthday has not yet occurred in the current year, one
								year is removed from that total.
							</p>
							<p>
								It then compares the months. When the current day number is earlier
								than the birth-day number, the calculation borrows the actual number
								of days from the previous calendar month. That is important because a
								month can contain 28, 29, 30 or 31 days.
							</p>
							<div class="guide-formula">
								<span>Exact age</span>
								<strong>completed years + completed months + remaining days</strong>
							</div>
						</article>

						<article class="guide-card">
							<div class="guide-number" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
							<h3>Why month length matters</h3>
							<p>
								Calendar months are not interchangeable units. February is shorter
								than March, while April, June, September and November have 30 days and
								several other months have 31. An exact calendar age must use the real
								month boundaries rather than assuming that every month lasts 30 days.
							</p>
							<p>
								This is also why an exact result such as “26 years, 4 months and 9
								days” is different from taking total days lived and dividing by an
								average month length. Both numbers can be useful, but they describe
								time in different ways.
							</p>
						</article>

						<article id="leap-years" class="guide-card guide-card-wide">
							<div class="guide-number" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
							<div>
								<h3>Leap years and February 29 birthdays</h3>
								<p>
									The Gregorian calendar is about 365.2425 days long on average, so
									most years contain 365 days and leap years contain 366. The usual
									rule is that a year divisible by four is a leap year. Century years
									are the exception: they must also be divisible by 400. That is why
									2000 was a leap year, while 1900 was not.
								</p>
								<p>
									If you were born on February 29, your recorded birth date remains
									February 29 even though that date does not appear every year. This
									calculator preserves the leap-day birth date in the age calculation.
									For the next-birthday countdown in a non-leap year, it uses February
									28 so the countdown has a practical annual date rather than skipping
									straight to the next leap year.
								</p>
								<div class="guide-note">
									<strong>Example:</strong>
									A person born on February 29, 2000 still has a leap-day birth
									date. Their exact age advances normally each year; February 29 is
									simply the special calendar date on which they were born.
								</div>
							</div>
						</article>

						<article id="age-units" class="guide-card">
							<div class="guide-number" aria-hidden="true">05</div>
							<h3>Age in years, months, weeks and days</h3>
							<p>
								Different age units are useful in different situations. Years are the
								standard way to describe a person's age. Months are especially useful
								for babies and short time spans. Weeks and days are useful when you
								want a more precise sense of how much time has passed.
							</p>
							<p>
								The lifetime statistics on this page also show hours, minutes and
								seconds. Those totals are based on elapsed time and continue updating
								while the results are open. The “total months” statistic is an
								approximate conversion using the average Gregorian month length,
								whereas the large age result uses true calendar months.
							</p>
						</article>

						<article class="guide-card">
							<div class="guide-number" aria-hidden="true">06</div>
							<h3>Why your age changes throughout the day</h3>
							<p>
								Your age in completed years changes on your birthday, but your time
								lived never stops increasing. Every second adds another second to the
								lifetime total. That is why this calculator separates calendar age
								from live elapsed-time statistics.
							</p>
							<p>
								The calculation intentionally uses the local date and time on your
								device. Around midnight, two people in different time zones can
								therefore see a different current date for a short period. That is
								normal: birthdays and calendar dates are local experiences.
							</p>
						</article>
					</div>

					<section id="birth-traditions-guide" class="traditions-guide">
						<div class="traditions-guide-heading">
							<p class="card-label">Beyond the Gregorian date</p>
							<h3>One birthday, several traditions</h3>
							<p>
								A single Gregorian birth date can be represented or interpreted in
								different ways. The calculator keeps those traditions separate so
								you can see each result without confusing it with your exact age.
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="traditions-explainer-grid">
							<article>
								<span class="tradition-mark" aria-hidden="true">☾</span>
								<h4>Hijri birth date</h4>
								<p>
									The Islamic Hijri calendar is lunar, with months tied to lunar
									cycles rather than the solar year used by the Gregorian calendar.
									A Hijri year is therefore shorter than a Gregorian year, so the same
									Gregorian birthday does not stay on the same Hijri date each year.
								</p>
								<p>
									Different Islamic calendar methods can occasionally place a date one
									day apart. Regional moon sighting, tabular calculations and calendar
									implementations can all contribute to that difference. The date shown
									here comes from the Islamic calendar support in your browser.
								</p>
							</article>

							<article>
								<span class="tradition-mark" aria-hidden="true">✦</span>
								<h4>Western zodiac sign</h4>
								<p>
									Western astrology commonly divides the year into twelve zodiac
									signs. The star-sign result on this page is based on the conventional
									date ranges for Aries, Taurus, Gemini, Cancer, Leo, Virgo, Libra,
									Scorpio, Sagittarius, Capricorn, Aquarius and Pisces.
								</p>
								<p>
									The result is provided as a cultural and entertainment feature. It
									does not affect the mathematical age calculation, and the descriptive
									traits should not be treated as scientific measurements of
									personality.
								</p>
							</article>

							<article>
								<span class="tradition-mark" aria-hidden="true">◎</span>
								<h4>Chinese zodiac and element</h4>
								<p>
									The Chinese zodiac uses a twelve-animal cycle together with a
									five-element cycle. Crucially, the Chinese zodiac year does not
									change on January 1. A person born in January or early February may
									belong to the previous Chinese calendar year.
								</p>
								<p>
									For that reason, this calculator asks the browser for the Chinese
									calendar year corresponding to the actual birth date instead of using
									the Gregorian year alone. The optional Muslim preference changes only
									how one animal label is presented; it does not change the element or
									the underlying calendar-year calculation.
								</p>
							</article>
						</div>
					</section>

